    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a loft addition for a residential project up in Oregon. I'm looking at using a Douglas Fir beam for the main span. The span is 14 feet and I'm expecting a dead load of about 15 psf plus a live load of 30 psf. Can you help me figure out what size beam I'll need to keep deflection within L/360 limits?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a 14-foot span with those loads, you'll need to calculate the required section modulus to keep deflection under L/360. Since you're using Douglas Fir, you'll need to consult the relevant span tables or perform a full beam design calculation using the allowable stress design (ASD) or load and resistance factor design (LRFD) methods. Generally, for a 14-foot span under those loads, a substantial beam size will be necessary to control deflection, which is often the governing factor in residential loft additions. You should definitely check the specific design values for Douglas Fir in Oregon for the most accurate sizing.",
