    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i'm trying to avoid just guessing with tables though. if i'm looking at a standard 4x12 or maybe a double 2x12, do you think those would be enough for the deflection limit? also, i'm a bit worried about any internal knots or stuff in the wood that might mess with the load capacity. how much of a margin should i build in for those kind of hidden imperfections?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those sizes, a 4x12 or even a double 2x12, are likely going to be on the smaller side for a 14-foot span under those combined loads, especially when deflection is the strict limit. You're right to be concerned about knots; they are the very reason why lumber grades exist, which is how the lumber industry manages the risk of hidden imperfections. It's much safer to design based on the specified *design values* for the lumber grade you purchase, rather than trying to guess a \"margin\" for knots.\n\nWhen you specify a certain grade of Douglas Fir, like No. 2 or Select Structural, the engineering tables already factor in a reduction for expected defects. If you are worried about *unexpected* defects, you are essentially asking for a safety factor beyond what the code requires, which is risky in construction. It's better to select a larger nominal size, like a 6x12 or even a built-up girder, which provides more material redundancy against localized defects.\n\nI always remind my clients that a structural engineer is the only one who can sign off on this, as they can account for the specific grade and moisture content of the wood you procure",
      "source": "target",
