    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. i'll look more into the 6x12 option. just one more thing—when i'm inspecting the timber on site, is there anything specific i should look for besides obvious knots? sometimes you get these weird, small inconsistencies or things deep inside the grain that aren't super obvious but still feel like they could compromise the strength later on. how do you account for those \"unseen\" issues during inspection?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When inspecting timber on site, beyond the obvious knots, you should look for checks, splits, or radial cracks running along the grain, even if they are small. These are often the hidden vulnerabilities, as they can act like stress risers, especially under cyclical loading, and they are much more insidious than a surface knot. You also want to check for signs of decay or insect damage, which can be nearly invisible until they compromise the cross-section.\n\nAccountability for these \"unseen\" issues is precisely why the lumber grading system exists, which is a highly standardized process. If you are using lumber that has been properly graded and stamped, you are relying on the mill's testing procedures to have already accounted for those internal flaws. If you are sourcing rough-sawn or un-graded timber, you are essentially taking on the full risk of those hidden flaws, which is a dangerous gamble for a main support beam.\n\nIt's a delicate balance, but if you are concerned about hidden issues, the most robust solution is to specify a larger, higher-grade beam, which naturally provides more material to absorb the shock of any unexpected flaw. If you find a piece that looks questionable, even if it seems fine, it'",
